{"abstract":[{"text":"Teach the parser words the built-in table doesn’t know.","type":"text"}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/AddingCustomVerbs"},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/gnusto\/addingcustomverbs"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto"]]},"metadata":{"title":"Adding Custom Verbs","roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","modules":[{"name":"Gnusto"}]},"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"sections":[],"kind":"article","seeAlsoSections":[{"generated":true,"title":"Adding Vocabulary","anchor":"Adding-Vocabulary","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/StubVerbs","doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/verb(_:_:)","doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxRule","doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement"]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Gnusto ships with a standard verb table in two tiers. The ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"core","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" tier is verbs the engine backs with real behavior: ","type":"text"},{"code":"take","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"drop"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"examine"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"wear"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"go","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":"follow","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"greet"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"look"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"wait"},{"text":" (","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"z"},{"type":"text","text":"), and their synonyms. The "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"stub"}]},{"type":"text","text":" tier is verbs the parser knows as "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"words"}]},{"type":"text","text":" with no mechanic behind them — "},{"code":"attack","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"dig","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"smell","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"climb","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"jump","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"buy","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"pray","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"xyzzy","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and some forty more — each answering with one line of stock prose. See ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/StubVerbs","type":"reference"},{"text":" for what they are and how to give one real behavior.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"When your game needs a verb neither tier covers — ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ring"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"wind"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"chime"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"barter"},{"type":"text","text":" — you declare it once with "},{"code":"#verb","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and handle it in a rule."}]},{"inlineContent":[{"code":"wait","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (and its alias ","type":"text"},{"code":"z","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":") is a normal, time-passing turn: it prints the "},{"code":"timePasses","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" line (“Time passes.”) and lets fuses and daemons tick — the standard way to let a countdown run down or a wandering monster catch up. Re-skin the line by mutating ","type":"text"},{"code":"text.timePasses","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"There are three beats to a custom verb: "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"declare"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":" it, "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"list"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" it in a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"verbs"},{"type":"text","text":" block, and "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"respond"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" to it in a rule.","type":"text"}]},{"text":"Declare the verb","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Declare-the-verb"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb"},{"type":"text","text":" lives inside an "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"extension Intent"},{"type":"text","text":" — that placement is what makes the leading-dot spelling ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".ring"},{"type":"text","text":") work everywhere an "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Intent"},{"type":"text","text":" is expected. Its first argument names the intent; each argument after that is one complete "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"pattern"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the words the player types, in order, with slots where noun phrases (or a direction) go."}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift","code":["extension Intent {","    #verb(\"ring\", [\"ring\", .directObject])","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"This generates a typed constant, "},{"code":"Intent.ring","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", that carries its verb row. String literals in a pattern are literal words; the slots are "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement\/directObject"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement\/indirectObject","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement\/direction","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement\/topic","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":". With no pattern at all, the verb is the name: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb(\"chime\")"},{"type":"text","text":" accepts a bare "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"chime"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Pick a name the engine doesn’t already own. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb"},{"text":" mints ","type":"text"},{"code":"Intent.<name>","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and a second declaration of a name the engine ships makes ","type":"text"},{"code":".<name>","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ambiguous in any file that imports both modules — so ","type":"text"},{"code":"#verb(\"dig\")","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" in a library is a trap, while ","type":"text"},{"code":"#verb(\"excavate\", [\"dig\", .directObject])","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is fine.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Patterns are validated as you type — a malformed shape is a compile-time error, with the same wording the bootstrap uses for hand-built rows."}]},{"text":"List it, then respond to it","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"List-it-then-respond-to-it"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The rows reach the parser through your game’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"verbs","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" block, which splices everything a listed intent carries. A rule keyed on the same constant gives the verb behavior — a custom intent has no built-in default action, so any noun your rules don’t cover falls through to ","type":"text"},{"code":"text.cantDoThat","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (“You can’t do that.”), and costs no turn, exactly as a parse error costs none:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["struct Temple: Game {","    let bell = Item { name(\"brass bell\") }","","    var verbs: [SyntaxRule] {","        .ring","    }","","    var rules: Rules {","        bell.before(.ring) {","            try reply(\"The bell tolls, deep and sonorous. Somewhere, a door unlatches.\")","        }","    }","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"List several intents as one array — bare "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".ring"},{"type":"text","text":" statements on consecutive lines would parse as a single chained member access:"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["var verbs: [SyntaxRule] {","    [.ring, .polish, .chime]","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"If you forget the listing, the rule silently never fires from typed input; the bootstrap records a non-fatal warning naming the intent and the fix. It warns about the mirror mistake too — a verb you list and then wire to nothing, which the parser will match and stage 4 will have no answer for.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Because the object resolves into ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/directObject","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":", you can attach the rule to the object (","type":"text"},{"code":"bell.before(…)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") or handle the intent more broadly on the ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/World","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" when several objects share behavior:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","code":["world.before(.ring) {","    guard let thing = command.directObject else {","        try refuse(\"Ring what?\")","    }","    try reply(\"You ring the \\(thing.name). Nothing happens.\")","}"],"type":"codeListing"},{"level":2,"anchor":"Shape-the-pattern","type":"heading","text":"Shape the pattern"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A pattern reads the way it’s typed. Some shapes, from the standard table and beyond:"}]},{"rows":[[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Pattern","type":"text"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Player types"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Command gets"}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb(\"chime\")"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"chime"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"just the intent"}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"#verb(\"tunnel\", [\"tunnel\", .direction])","type":"codeVoice"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tunnel down"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"a "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/direction","isActive":true}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb(\"ring\", [\"ring\", .directObject])"}]}],[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"ring bell","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"a ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/directObject","type":"reference"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb(\"wind\", [\"wind\", .directObject, \"up\"])"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"wind canary up"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"a direct object","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"#verb(\"barter\", [\"barter\", .directObject, \"for\", .indirectObject])","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"barter coin for rope"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"direct + indirect objects"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb(\"peek\", [\"look\", \"under\", .directObject])"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"look under rug"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"a direct object","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb(\"ask\", [\"ask\", .directObject, \"about\", .topic])"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"ask monk about the bell","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"a direct object + a ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/topic"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb(\"shove\", [\"push\", .directObject, .direction])"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"push the sandstone wall north","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"a direct object + a direction","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]]],"header":"row","type":"table"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The rules follow from one question: ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"where does a noun phrase end?"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" A literal word and a direction slot take exactly one token each; the object and topic slots take as many as the sentence gives them. So an object slot can end wherever everything behind it has a fixed width — the phrase stops that many tokens from the end of the line — and where it doesn’t, a literal word has to close it. That, plus: a pattern starts with at least one literal word (the verb); at most one direct-object and one indirect-object slot, direct first; at most one direction slot, since a second would overwrite the first; and a topic slot ends its pattern and never mixes with a second object or a direction.","type":"text"}]},{"level":3,"type":"heading","text":"A noun and a direction","anchor":"A-noun-and-a-direction"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"[\"push\", .directObject, .direction]","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is the shape for a verb that needs both. It works because a direction slot takes exactly one token, so the split is fixed: the noun phrase is everything up to the last token. That phrase resolves like any other, so adjectives, synonyms, pronouns and disambiguation all reach it — "},{"code":"push the sandstone wall north","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"code":"push it north","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" both arrive with "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/directObject","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" set."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The direction need not be the last thing in the pattern, and the noun need not stand immediately before it. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[\"hurl\", .directObject, \"at\", .direction]"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[\"wedge\", .directObject, .direction, \"hard\"]"},{"text":" are two tokens of fixed width behind the noun instead of one, and split the same way.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Leave the direction off and the row asks for it: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"push the sandstone wall"},{"type":"text","text":" answers "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"“Which way do you want to push the sandstone wall?”","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":", and the next line completes the command. Leave the noun off too and the bare verb asks for the noun, exactly as "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"push <object>"},{"type":"text","text":" does — this shape displaces nothing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Its narrower sibling is a "},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"literal word"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":" beside the direction slot, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[\"tunnel\", \"shaft\", .direction]"},{"type":"text","text":". That still works, and stays the right choice where the direction is the whole of the meaning and the noun is decoration. But the word is matched, never resolved: "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/directObject","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" stays nil, the rule cannot tell which shaft was named, and adjectives, synonyms and disambiguation all stop at the pattern. It also costs one row per spelling."}]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The two coexist on one intent, most specific first, which is how a game buys a fixed phrasing and a general one at once:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["extension Intent {","    #verb(\"shove\",","          [\"push\", .direction],                 \/\/ push north","          [\"push\", \"wall\", .direction],         \/\/ push wall north — no object bound","          [\"push\", .directObject, .direction])  \/\/ push the sandstone wall north","}"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"And a direction slot with nothing left to fill it still "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"succeeds"}]},{"type":"text","text":", with a nil direction — that is the branch that lets bare "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"go"},{"type":"text","text":" ask “Which way?”. So a row like "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[\"push\", .direction]"},{"type":"text","text":" means bare "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"push"},{"type":"text","text":" reaches your intent instead of the built-in’s “What do you want to push?”, and your rule has to answer it. The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"<object> <direction>"},{"type":"text","text":" shape never does this: it asks for whichever half is missing, and where the line is a bare direction it stands aside so a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[\"push\", .direction]"},{"type":"text","text":" row for the same verb can take it."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"topic"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is the odd one out, and deliberately so. The object slots resolve against what the player can see, and refuse anything else — which is right for things and wrong for subjects. A topic instead takes the rest of the line as typed, normalized but never looked up, so "},{"code":"ask the monk about zeppelins","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" reaches the monk’s rules and lets him shrug rather than dying in the parser as “You can’t see any such thing.” It arrives as a "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Topic"},{"type":"text","text":" on "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/topic"},{"type":"text","text":", with the words already lowercased, stripped of punctuation and filler; "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Topic\/normalize(_:)"},{"type":"text","text":" puts an author’s own keyword through the same mill so the two can be compared. The line exactly as typed is still on "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/rawInput","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Several patterns on one ","type":"text"},{"code":"#verb","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" share the intent — that is how synonyms and alternate word orders work:","type":"text"}]},{"code":["extension Intent {","    #verb(\"ring\",","          [\"ring\", .directObject],","          [\"sound\", .directObject])","    #verb(\"light\",","          [\"turn\", \"on\", .directObject],","          [\"turn\", .directObject, \"on\"])","}"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Among rows sharing a verb word, the parser tries the most specific pattern first (more literal words, then more slots); ties keep their table order. The literal word sealing the direct object ahead of an indirect slot arrives on "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/preposition"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"text":"A preposition’s synonyms come free","level":3,"type":"heading","anchor":"A-prepositions-synonyms-come-free"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Write a row’s preposition once. ","type":"text"},{"code":"in","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" also answers to ","type":"text"},{"code":"inside","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"into","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and ","type":"text"},{"code":"on","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" to ","type":"text"},{"code":"onto","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"upon","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", wherever the literal stands in the pattern — in the verb-identifying run (","type":"text"},{"code":"look in","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"), closing an object slot (","type":"text"},{"code":"put <object> in <second object>","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"), or as a trailing particle (","type":"text"},{"code":"turn <object> on","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"). So a single ","type":"text"},{"code":"[\"pour\", .directObject, \"in\", .indirectObject]","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" accepts ","type":"text"},{"code":"pour water into the sack","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and a second row spelling out ","type":"text"},{"code":"into","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" buys nothing.","type":"text"}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Two things the table deliberately does not do. It never reaches noun resolution, so a game may still name a thing "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"inside pocket"},{"type":"text","text":" — and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"put the inside pocket in the box"},{"type":"text","text":" splits at the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"in"},{"type":"text","text":" the player typed, because a literal closing a slot looks for its own word before it looks for a synonym. And it is not the direction table: a bare "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"in"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"inside"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"out"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"outside"},{"type":"text","text":" is still a way to walk."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/preposition","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":" carries the ","type":"text"},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"pattern’s","type":"text"}]},{"text":" word, not the typed one, so a rule reading it sees ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"in"},{"text":" however the player spelled it.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Reclaiming-a-built-in-verb","text":"Reclaiming a built-in verb","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A verb table merges the built-in rows, each content bundle’s, each plugin’s, and the game’s own, under a ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"last-wins"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" policy keyed on what the player types (the full pattern). If your pattern exactly matches a ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"core"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" row, yours reclaims it — the parser emits ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"your","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" intent instead — and the engine logs a non-fatal warning so the override is never silent. The intent name doesn’t have to match the typed word, which is what makes a reclaim readable:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["extension Intent {","    #verb(\"steal\", [\"take\", .directObject])   \/\/ `take coin` now means stealing","}"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Reclaiming a ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"stub"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" row is silent, because a stub has no behavior to shadow and overriding it is the expected end state. That is the whole point of the two tiers: the warning catches you accidentally hiding something real, and stays quiet when you’re filling in a blank. See ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/StubVerbs","type":"reference"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A few words a game is ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"likely","type":"text"}]},{"text":" to want are deliberately absent from both tiers. Bare ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"hello"},{"text":" and bare ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"hi"},{"text":" are the clearest case: they are the kind of one-word verb a game likes to own outright — Zork 1 does — and even a silent reclaim is worse than leaving them free, because the engine would have to pick a greeting line for everybody. The engine ships ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"greet <object>"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"hello <object>"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"hi <object>"},{"text":", and leaves the bare forms to ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GnustoConversation"},{"text":" or to you. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ring"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"wind"},{"text":" are out for the same reason: a game that has a bell wants to say what ringing it does.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"The-substrate-raw-SyntaxRule","text":"The substrate: raw `SyntaxRule`"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"#verb"},{"text":" expands to a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"static let"},{"text":" whose ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Intent"},{"text":" carries ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxRule","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" rows — the same rows you can build by hand when a table is genuinely dynamic:","type":"text"}]},{"code":["var verbs: [SyntaxRule] {","    SyntaxRule(\"ring\", .directObject, intent: Intent(\"ring\"))","}"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The two forms interoperate: an ","type":"text"},{"code":"Intent(\"ring\")","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" built from a string matches a ","type":"text"},{"code":"#verb","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"-minted ","type":"text"},{"code":".ring","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" everywhere (the rows an intent carries are not part of its identity). Hand-built rows are validated by the bootstrap at launch instead of at compile time.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Where verbs can come from","anchor":"Where-verbs-can-come-from","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"verbs"},{"type":"text","text":" block exists on more than the game type. A "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/GameContent","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" bundle and a "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/GamePlugin","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" each carry their own "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"verbs"},{"type":"text","text":", all merged into one table at startup. That is how a reusable plugin ships a whole verb — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GnustoMeleeCombat"},{"type":"text","text":" promotes the engine’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"attack"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"kill"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"hit"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"fight"},{"type":"text","text":" stubs to real combat with an "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"actions"},{"type":"text","text":" row, and adds the two rows the engine doesn’t ship ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"stab … with …"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"strike … with …","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") to the same intent. See ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Plugins"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Engine intents list the same way your own do. A "},{"code":"#verb","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" intent carries its rows on the constant; an engine intent keeps its rows in the standard table, and listing it splices those. Rows you’re adding to that intent go alongside, spelled as "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxRule","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":" values — which is what melee’s "},{"code":"verbs","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" block is:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["public var verbs: [SyntaxRule] {","    .attack                                                                     \/\/ the engine's rows","    SyntaxRule(\"stab\", .directObject, \"with\", .indirectObject, intent: .attack)","    SyntaxRule(\"strike\", .directObject, \"with\", .indirectObject, intent: .attack)","}"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Reclaiming a built-in shape ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"for the intent that already held it"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" — which is what listing an engine intent does — isn’t an override, so it doesn’t warn. Reclaiming one for a different intent still does.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"See-also","text":"See also"},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/StubVerbs"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/WritingRules"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/TheTurnPipeline","isActive":true}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Plugins","type":"reference","isActive":true}],"type":"paragraph"}]}]}],"kind":"content"}],"references":{"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/SyntaxElement/topic":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"topic"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement\/topic","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"An abstract subject of conversation — “ask the butler about ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"murder","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":"”. Unlike the object slots, a topic is never resolved against","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the world: it takes the rest of the line as typed, so a subject the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"game has never heard of still reaches the rules instead of dying as","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"“You can’t see any such thing.”","type":"text"}],"title":"SyntaxElement.topic","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/syntaxelement\/topic","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/Command/directObject":{"fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"directObject","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Item","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:6Gnusto4ItemV"},{"kind":"text","text":"?"}],"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/command\/directobject","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/directObject","title":"directObject","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The primary item the command acts on, if any."}],"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","type":"topic"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/SyntaxRule":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"SyntaxRule","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"SyntaxRule","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxRule","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"One row of the verb table: a pattern of literal words and slots, and the","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"intent a match produces. Data, not code — games can add rows through their","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"verbs","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" block to teach the parser new player-typeable verbs.","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/syntaxrule","type":"topic","title":"SyntaxRule","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/verb(_:_:)":{"fragments":[{"text":"macro","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"verb","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"(","kind":"text"},{"text":"String","preciseIdentifier":"s:SS","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":", [","kind":"text"},{"text":"SyntaxElement","preciseIdentifier":"s:6Gnusto13SyntaxElementO","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"]...)","kind":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/verb(_:_:)","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"Declares a custom player-typeable verb in one place: a typed intent","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"constant plus the verb rows that produce it.","type":"text"}],"title":"verb(_:_:)","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/verb(_:_:)","type":"topic","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/Command/direction":{"abstract":[{"text":"The direction the player named, if any.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/direction","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"identifier","text":"direction"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:6Gnusto9DirectionO","text":"Direction","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol","title":"direction","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/command\/direction"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/Command/rawInput":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"rawInput"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:SS","text":"String"}],"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/command\/rawinput","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/rawInput","abstract":[{"text":"The full line the player typed.","type":"text"}],"title":"rawInput","role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","type":"topic"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/WritingRules":{"kind":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/WritingRules","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Attach game logic to items, locations, and the world."}],"title":"Writing Rules","role":"article","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/writingrules"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/SyntaxElement/indirectObject":{"abstract":[{"text":"The secondary noun phrase.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement\/indirectObject","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"indirectObject","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol","title":"SyntaxElement.indirectObject","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/syntaxelement\/indirectobject"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/Intent":{"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/intent","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Intent","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"A canonical action the player can attempt. Games can mint their own —","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"most readably with ","type":"text"},{"code":"#verb","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", or directly (","type":"text"},{"code":"Intent(\"ring\")","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") — and register","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"verbs and default behavior for them.","type":"text"}],"type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Intent","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","title":"Intent","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Intent","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/TheTurnPipeline":{"abstract":[{"text":"What happens between the player pressing Return and the next prompt.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/TheTurnPipeline","kind":"article","type":"topic","role":"article","title":"The Turn Pipeline","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/theturnpipeline"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/Topic/normalize(_:)":{"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/topic\/normalize(_:)","title":"normalize(_:)","type":"topic","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"static"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"normalize"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SS","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"String"},{"kind":"text","text":") -> ["},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SS","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"String"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Topic\/normalize(_:)","kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"Normalizes an author-written keyword exactly as the parser normalizes","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"what the player typed, so a topic table can match the two word for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"word.","type":"text"}]},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/Plugins":{"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/plugins","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Plugins","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Package a reusable game system as an importable unit of verbs and rules."}],"title":"Plugins","role":"article","kind":"article","type":"topic"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/GameContent":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"GameContent","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"GameContent","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/GameContent","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A self-contained slice of a game’s world: its own rooms, items, and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Global"},{"type":"text","text":" state, plus the geography, rules, and verbs that go with them."}],"title":"GameContent","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/gamecontent","type":"topic","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/SyntaxElement/direction":{"fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"direction","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement\/direction","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"A compass direction.","type":"text"}],"title":"SyntaxElement.direction","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/syntaxelement\/direction","type":"topic","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/World":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"text":"World","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/world","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/World","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Rules that apply everywhere, declared through the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"world"},{"type":"text","text":" identifier in a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"rules"},{"type":"text","text":" block:"}],"title":"World","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"World","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","type":"topic"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/StubVerbs":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The verbs the parser knows as words even where your game has no mechanic."}],"kind":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/StubVerbs","type":"topic","role":"article","title":"Stub Verbs","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/stubverbs"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/Command/topic":{"fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"topic","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:6Gnusto5TopicV","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Topic"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/topic","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"What the player wants to talk about, for verbs with a topic slot."}],"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/command\/topic","type":"topic","title":"topic","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto":{"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto","title":"Gnusto","abstract":[{"text":"A Swift engine for writing parser-driven interactive fiction — text adventures in the tradition of Zork and Infocom’s ZIL.","type":"text"}],"role":"collection","kind":"symbol","type":"topic"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/SyntaxElement/directObject":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement\/directObject","kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"The primary noun phrase.","type":"text"}],"fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"directObject","kind":"identifier"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol","title":"SyntaxElement.directObject","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/syntaxelement\/directobject"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/Command/preposition":{"title":"preposition","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Command\/preposition","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The preposition the player used, if any."}],"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/command\/preposition","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"preposition"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:SS","text":"String"},{"kind":"text","text":"?"}],"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/GamePlugin":{"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/gameplugin","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/GamePlugin","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A reusable game system — commerce, combat, dialog, magic — packaged as an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"importable unit of "},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"verbs","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"rules","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":", that a host game opts into."}],"type":"topic","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"GamePlugin","kind":"identifier"}],"kind":"symbol","title":"GamePlugin","fragments":[{"text":"protocol","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"GamePlugin","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/SyntaxElement":{"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"SyntaxElement","kind":"identifier"}],"fragments":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"SyntaxElement","kind":"identifier"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/SyntaxElement","role":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"One element of a verb pattern: a literal word the player must type, or a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"slot the parser fills from the rest of the sentence. String literals in a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pattern are ","type":"text"},{"code":".word","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"s, so rows read the way they’re typed:","type":"text"}],"title":"SyntaxElement","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/syntaxelement","kind":"symbol"},"doc://Gnusto/documentation/Gnusto/Topic":{"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Topic","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/gnusto\/topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Gnusto\/documentation\/Gnusto\/Topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"What the player wants to talk about: the words after “about”, normalized"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the way the parser normalizes everything else — lowercased, punctuation","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"dropped, filler stripped.","type":"text"}],"title":"Topic","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"Topic","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","type":"topic"}}}